{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>And the captain of the slaughterers (שר הטבחים).</b> [This means] the slaughterers of the king's animals — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 37:36). And likewise, \"And the slaughterer (הטבח) set it\" (I Samuel 9:24), \"for perfumers and for cooks (ולטבחות)\" (I Samuel 8:13). But the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 37:36) is more plausible, since the prison house was in his house, and we find the term טביחה used for the killing of men: \"Prepare a slaughter (מטבח) for his sons\" (Isaiah 14:21), \"You have slaughtered (טבחת) and shown no pity\" (Lamentations 2:21). And a proof for this matter is what is written in Daniel (2:14): \"to Arioch, the chief slaughterer (רב טבחיא) of the king, who had gone out to kill the wise men of Babylon.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:26.728970911Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}